<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of the Monongahela</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1755">1755</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Braddock's Defeat · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of the Monongahela was fought on 9 July 1755 near the Monongahela River, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-duquesne">Fort Duquesne</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a>. A British and colonial army under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-braddock">Edward Braddock</a>, marching to take the fort, was ambushed by a smaller force of French troops and Native allies and lost about two-thirds of its men, and Braddock was mortally wounded. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> helped lead the retreat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-monongahela.jpg" alt="Battle of the Monongahela" width="320" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques Auguste Regnier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Washington_the_soldier.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-braddock">Edward Braddock</a> came to the Thirteen Colonies as the first Commander-in-Chief, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, with the 44th and 48th Regiments from Ireland, and recruited provincial troops until he had roughly 2,200 men when he left Fort Cumberland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>, on 29 May. His expedition, one of four attacks planned against the French, was to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-duquesne">Fort Duquesne</a> at the forks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a> and then go on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-niagara">Fort Niagara</a>. Scornful of the need for Indian scouts, he took only eight Mingo guides, and the road had to be widened constantly for artillery and wagons. He split his army and pushed ahead with a flying column of 1,300.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-duquesne">Fort Duquesne</a> had just been reinforced. Its commander, Claude-Pierre Pecaudy de Contrecœur, sent Captain Daniel Liénard de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaujeu">Beaujeu</a> with about 800 men, 108 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troupes-de-la-marine">Troupes de la Marine</a>, 146 militia and 600 Indians, to check the British advance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The column crossed the Monongahela on 9 July and met the French force head on. The advance guard under Lieutenant-Colonel Thomas Gage opened fire and killed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beaujeu">Beaujeu</a>, but Captain Dumas rallied the French, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odawa">Odawa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ojibwe">Ojibwe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potawatomi">Potawatomi</a> warriors spread through the woods on both flanks. Gage's men fell back into the main body on the narrow road, and the regulars, keeping tight formations, made easy targets; several platoons fired on each other and on provincials sheltering behind trees. Only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> rearguard fought effectively from cover.</p><p>After three hours Braddock was shot in the lung, possibly by one of his own men, and resistance collapsed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>, who held no official command, formed a rear guard that let the survivors escape. That night about a dozen captured soldiers were tortured and burned to death, as the prisoner James Smith witnessed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Of about 1,300 men, 456 were killed and 422 wounded; of 86 officers, 26 were killed and 37 wounded; the French and Canadians reported 23 killed and 20 wounded. Braddock died on 13 July and was buried on the road near Fort Necessity. Colonel Thomas Dunbar burned about 150 wagons and retreated towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a>. The defeat led the British to make far better use of skirmishers, often armed with rifles, and historians still debate whether Braddock lost by keeping to European methods or by failing to apply them. The French held the Ohio Country until the Forbes Expedition forced them out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-duquesne">Fort Duquesne</a> in 1758.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of the Monongahela” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Seven Years War, French and Native forces defeated General Edward Braddock near the <b>Monongahela</b> in 1755, and the Marquis de Montcalm took Fort Oswego in 1756 and Fort William Henry in 1757 and won at Carillon in 1758.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-france">New France · Military</a></li></ul></section>
